#: setup-env.sh:525
msgid "  Running inside a container, so Docker's own ranges cannot be listed from here. Excluding 172.16.0.0/12 instead, which covers them. If your LAN genuinely lives there, type the address rather than picking from the list."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:527
msgid "Could not ask Docker which ranges it has allocated - is it installed and running? Falling back to excluding 172.16.0.0/12 entirely. If your LAN genuinely lives there, type the address rather than picking from the list."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:760
msgid "The address printers use to reach this server."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:762
msgid "  This is the one setting with no sensible default, and the one that is expensive to get wrong: it is written into every USB provisioning bundle, and the printer certificate is issued once - on the first start - covering this address and every address the machine can see. Set it now and it is covered by construction."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:776
msgid "  Addresses on this machine that a printer could reach:"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:787
msgid "    $n) $addr"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:795
msgid "  A name keeps working when this machine's address changes - an address does not - but only while your router keeps publishing it, which is not checked here. Test it from another machine before relying on it."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:816
msgid "No usable address detected on this machine. Loopback, link-local and Docker's own ranges are excluded - a printer cannot reach any of them, and one baked into the certificate cannot be corrected without a reissue."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:828
msgid "Left unset. USB-key provisioning will refuse to produce a snippet until it is."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:834
msgid "$host is this machine talking to itself - no printer can reach it."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:835
msgid "  Use it anyway"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:854
msgid "  $host is served by ordinary DNS, not mDNS - a printer can resolve it."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:857
msgid "$host is probably an mDNS name, and Prusa firmware announces itself over mDNS but cannot resolve it - so a printer would never find this server, however well the name works from your own machine. There is no dig, nslookup or host here to check whether your network serves .local from real DNS instead, which some older ones do."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:858
msgid "  Use it anyway"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:861
msgid "$host is an mDNS name - it does not resolve in DNS, only by multicast. Prusa firmware announces itself over mDNS but cannot resolve it, so a printer given this name will never find this server, even though it resolves perfectly from your own machine. Use the address, or a name your router publishes in DNS."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:862
msgid "  Use it anyway"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:875
msgid "$host does not resolve from here. The certificate will cover the name, but a printer that cannot resolve it either will have nothing to connect to."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:876
msgid "  Use it anyway"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:878
msgid "  $host resolves to $resolved - the certificate will cover both."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:887
msgid "$host is inside $hit, which printers on your network cannot route to. It would be minted into the certificate and frozen there."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:888
msgid "  Use it anyway"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:946
msgid "The name people type in a browser. Cosmetic - it names the self-signed certificate, and a browser warns about that certificate whatever name it carries."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:952
msgid "The zone timestamps are shown in. The conversion happens on the server, so this decides what print history reads and what an invitation email says its expiry is."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1187
msgid "  Plain-HTTP visitors are redirected to HTTPS, and the redirect has to name the port a BROWSER should ask for - which is not necessarily the one published here, if anything forwards to this machine."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1193
msgid "Outgoing mail is optional. Without it, new accounts are created already confirmed and password reset is unavailable - invitations still work, you just pass the link on yourself."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1214
msgid "Homespool runs in a container, so $host is the container itself - not this machine."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1236
msgid "  Port $port is not one of the three well-known ones, so it does not say how the connection is encrypted. It has to match what the server offers."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1237
msgid "    1) STARTTLS - upgraded after connecting 2) implicit TLS - encrypted from the first byte 3) none - only for a server on this machine; sends the password in the clear"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1277
msgid "No source of randomness found - leaving the camera sidecar unauthenticated, which is what this deployment already had. Its port is not published."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1285
msgid "Generated a credential for the camera sidecar."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1325
msgid "Detected $address as the address printers reach this server on."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1351
msgid "/16 from 172.16 to 172.31 is taken. Set PROXY_SUBNET and PROXY_NETWORK by hand."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1356
msgid "$candidate instead."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1377
msgid "The compose network $subnet collides with:$colliding"
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1379
msgid "A collision with another Docker network fails loudly at startup. A collision with a route this machine already has does not: the stack comes up, and that network stops being reachable from here."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1383
msgid "Every /16 from 172.16 to 172.31 is in use here - pick a range by hand."
msgstr ""
#: setup-env.sh:1427
msgid "This stack has run before, so the printer certificate has already been issued - and it is issued once. Changing PRINTER_HOST in .env does not change what that certificate covers. Reissue it afterwards from Admin -> Printer certificate, or printers will fail to verify."
msgstr ""
